# The FoundationDB C ABI the store calls, and nothing else.
#
# Read `check_surface.sh` beside this file for how it is enforced. The list is checked
# against the symbols the built library actually leaves undefined, so it cannot go stale
# the way a comment can. `thirdparty/harness/iceoryx2.sigs` is the same idea for the bus.
#
# Why a list at all. `spec/Backend.lean` states seven laws and proves that what the layout
# needs is the laws and not FoundationDB, which is what lets a local backend carry the same
# store. That argument holds only while the C stays inside the surface the laws describe.
# One call to a feature with no equivalent underneath and the two backends stop being the
# same store — silently, and in a way no test here would notice.
#
# So this file is the whitelist half of that proof. A symbol absent from it is a decision,
# not an oversight, and the notes at the bottom say which decision.
#
# Format: one symbol per line. A comment starts with '#'.

# ── The client and its network thread ─────────────────────────────────────────
# One process, one network thread, one database handle. `weft_fdb_start` and
# `weft_fdb_stop`, which is the whole lifecycle.
fdb_select_api_version_impl
fdb_setup_network
fdb_run_network
fdb_stop_network
fdb_create_database
fdb_database_destroy

# ── Futures ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every future goes through `await`: block, then take the error. No callbacks, so no
# completion thread and nothing to reason about about ordering.
fdb_future_block_until_ready
fdb_future_get_error
fdb_future_get_value
fdb_future_get_keyvalue_array
fdb_future_destroy

# ── Transactions ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `run_txn` creates one, runs a body, and commits or retries. `fdb_transaction_on_error`
# is what decides whether an error may be retried and how long to wait before the next
# attempt, which is the one piece of retry policy this code does not own.
fdb_database_create_transaction
fdb_transaction_destroy
fdb_transaction_commit
fdb_transaction_on_error

# ── The keyspace ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Four operations, and they are the four in `Weft.Backend.Backend`. A range clear is one
# record whatever it removes, which is why the layout clears prefixes rather than keys.
fdb_transaction_get
fdb_transaction_get_range
fdb_transaction_set
fdb_transaction_clear_range

# ── What is deliberately absent ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each of these is a real FoundationDB feature with no SQLite equivalent. Adopting one
# means either the local backend emulates it — sound only because an actor is the single
# writer of its own store — or the two backends genuinely differ and `substitutable` in
# `spec/Backend.lean` stops being provable. That the code uses none of them is why a local
# backend is possible at all.
#
# fdb_transaction_set_option
#     The important one. It is how a caller would turn off read-your-writes, make reads
#     snapshot by default, or raise the transaction limits. Banning it is what pins the
#     code to default semantics, which is the claim `Backend.lean` is written against.
#
# fdb_transaction_watch
#     Superseded before it was ever wanted. A watch is a network round trip; iceoryx2 is
#     shared memory on one machine and a send is a pointer handoff. Any cross-store
#     notification is cheaper over the bus that is already there.
#
# fdb_transaction_atomic_op
#     The honest case is LOGN, where `resolve_body` reads a counter it is about to write.
#     An atomic add would save that read. It would also add a vtable concept the local
#     backend can only emulate with the read it was meant to remove.
#
# fdb_transaction_get_versionstamp
#     The strongest candidate, and still not taken. `weft/txn/NEXT` is a genuine hot key:
#     every store allocating a group txid conflicts with every other on it. But it is only
#     touched on group commits, `g_group_lock` already serialises those per process, and
#     the backend-neutral fix — allocate txids in blocks and hand them out in process —
#     removes nearly all of it without leaving the surface.
#
#     It does not close the txid reuse defect. A staging txid must be `head + 1`, because
#     `Weft.ParallelCommit.DbState.visible` is `tx ≤ head`. See
#     `loose_intent_accepts_a_dead_group`; the cheap fix there is clearing on abort.
#
# fdb_transaction_add_conflict_range, fdb_transaction_get_read_version,
# fdb_transaction_get_committed_version, fdb_transaction_clear, fdb_transaction_get_key,
# fdb_transaction_get_estimated_range_size_bytes, tenants, the directory and tuple layers
#     Unused. Conflict detection is implicit from serializable gets, which is what makes
#     `check_fence` a fence. The tuple layer is not used because `fdb_keys.h` is: a hand
#     rolled order preserving encoding that `fuzz/keys_test.cc` can test without linking
#     FoundationDB at all.
#
# ── One thing a symbol list cannot catch ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# A snapshot read is not a different symbol. It is the trailing argument to
# `fdb_transaction_get` and `fdb_transaction_get_range`, and it must stay zero. Reading the
# fence non-snapshot is what registers the read conflict that makes FoundationDB reject a
# write whose fence moved, so one snapshot read in the wrong place disables ownership and
# nothing fails loudly. `check_surface.sh` greps for it separately.
